  "description": "Write a Python function that takes two lists (arrays) as parameters and determines if the second list is a rotation of the first list (left or right).\n\nA rotation means that the elements are shifted circularly. For example, shifting [1, 2, 3] to the right by one position results in [3, 1, 2].\n\nThe function must return True if arr2 is a rotation of arr1, and False otherwise.\nIf the arrays have different lengths, they cannot be rotations of each other.\nTwo empty arrays are considered rotations of each other.",
  "prototype": "def array_rotation_detector(arr1: list, arr2: list) -> bool:",
  "function": "array_rotation_detector",
  "notes": [
    "File to submit: py_array_rotation_detector.py",
    "Forbidden: collections.deque.rotate()",
    "Return the value — do not print it."
  ],
